            <ArticleTitle>A preliminary economic evaluation of percutaneous neuromuscular electrical stimulation in the treatment of hemiplegic shoulder pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>645-51</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The objective of this study was to compare the cost-effectiveness of various treatment modalities for hemiplegic shoulder pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A stage II economic evaluation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Incremental cost effectiveness ratio of P-NMES, compared to slings and anti-inflammatory injections.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The incremental cost effectiveness ratio (ICER) of p-NMES, compared to anti-inflammatory injections is 6,061 euro(+/-3,285). The incremental cost of the first quality-adjusted life year after implantation of the P-NMES device compared to anti-inflammatory injections is 33,007 euro (+/-5,434). This decreases to approximately 7,000 euro after 5 years, and to approximately 5,000 euro after 10 survival years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In this early evaluation, P-NMES seems to be cost-effective according to known guidelines. Treatment with P-NMES is recommended for patients with chronic HSP.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
